WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.040
What if, what if the reason you haven't found your ultimate cosmic purpose in life

00:05.040 --> 00:07.680
is simply because the universe just forgot to write one for you?

00:08.240 --> 00:10.320
Right. Like it just slipped its mind entirely.

00:10.320 --> 00:15.920
Exactly. And what if realizing that is actually the most liberating news you will hear all year?

00:16.400 --> 00:22.640
I mean, if you've ever felt crushed by the pressure to figure out what you are meant to do.

00:22.640 --> 00:24.560
Which I think most of us have at some point.

00:24.560 --> 00:28.960
Oh, 100%. It feels like there's this golden ticket hidden somewhere.

00:28.960 --> 00:31.600
And you are just failing by not finding it.

00:32.320 --> 00:35.520
Well, this deep dive is going to take a massive weight off your shoulders.

00:35.520 --> 00:40.480
We really do carry around this heavy assumption that the objective meaning of our lives is

00:42.080 --> 00:44.640
it's like a hidden treasure waiting to be unearthed.

00:44.640 --> 00:48.080
We think if we just read the right book or I don't know, take the right job,

00:48.080 --> 00:49.040
find the right partner.

00:49.040 --> 00:52.240
The universe will suddenly click into place and validate our existence.

00:52.240 --> 00:52.880
Exactly.

00:53.520 --> 00:57.760
But the source material we were pulling apart today flips that entirely on its head.

00:57.840 --> 01:03.680
Yeah, we are getting into a deeply thought-provoking essay by Sangyi Yun.

01:03.680 --> 01:07.200
It's titled coming back to the human A sequel to Why Do We Live.

01:07.840 --> 01:11.440
And in the original piece, the author laid down a pretty radical premise, which is that

01:12.080 --> 01:14.000
meaning is not something you discover.

01:14.000 --> 01:15.600
It is something you create.

01:15.600 --> 01:16.640
Right. There is no script.

01:16.640 --> 01:17.600
No script at all.

01:17.600 --> 01:21.360
But I mean, that leaves us with a terrifying, massive blank page.

01:21.360 --> 01:24.000
Because if meaning is entirely up to us,

01:24.720 --> 01:26.640
what on earth should we build it around?

01:26.640 --> 01:28.320
That is the million dollar question.

01:28.320 --> 01:32.720
Yeah, if there's no cosmic rulebook, how do we decide what is actually worth caring about?

01:32.720 --> 01:34.320
Okay, let's unpack this.

01:34.320 --> 01:38.080
So to even begin answering what we should value,

01:38.080 --> 01:42.000
we have to look at the primary tool we use to figure things out, which is language.

01:42.800 --> 01:45.680
And we hit a wall almost immediately here.

01:45.680 --> 01:46.000
Right.

01:46.000 --> 01:49.760
Because the author points out that language actually fails us completely when we try to talk about

01:49.760 --> 01:50.560
ultimate meaning.

01:50.560 --> 01:50.880
Yeah.

01:50.880 --> 01:55.600
There's this famous line from the philosopher Lou Bigwick and Stein from his book The Trek Tattas,

01:55.680 --> 02:00.000
where he says, where of one cannot speak, there of one must be silent.

02:00.000 --> 02:03.360
Wow. That sounds like a, like a philosophical mic drop.

02:03.360 --> 02:04.240
It really is.

02:04.240 --> 02:09.280
You reach the edge of what words can accurately describe and you just have to shut up.

02:09.280 --> 02:11.840
But how does someone actually reach that edge?

02:11.840 --> 02:14.640
Because it feels like we have a word for literally everything.

02:14.640 --> 02:17.040
Well, we have words for the mechanics of the world, sure.

02:17.040 --> 02:17.600
Yeah.

02:17.600 --> 02:23.920
But thick and Stein was using pure, rigorous logic to find the boundary of language from the inside.

02:24.400 --> 02:27.200
Like imagine being in a massive dark room.

02:27.200 --> 02:28.800
Okay, I'm picturing it.

02:28.800 --> 02:36.560
Logic is you walking with your hands outstretched until you finally touch the cold hard wall of the room.

02:36.560 --> 02:38.160
You've mapped the interior.

02:38.160 --> 02:39.760
You know exactly where the language stops.

02:39.760 --> 02:40.240
Right.

02:40.240 --> 02:44.080
And you can point at the wall and say, there is something outside of this room,

02:44.080 --> 02:46.160
but my logic cannot describe it.

02:46.160 --> 02:47.360
But the catch is,

02:47.360 --> 02:48.880
Vic and Stein stays inside the room.

02:48.880 --> 02:50.080
Ah, see.

02:50.160 --> 02:53.280
The silence he advocates for is the silence of a magician

02:53.280 --> 02:55.360
who simply refuses to guess what's outside.

02:55.360 --> 02:56.880
He's like a cartographer.

02:56.880 --> 03:00.240
He draws this incredibly detailed map of a dense forest,

03:00.240 --> 03:04.160
walks right up to the edge of the parchment, points to the blank space off the paper and says,

03:04.800 --> 03:06.800
we can't go past here, the map is done.

03:06.800 --> 03:08.560
Yes, perfect analogy.

03:08.560 --> 03:10.560
But the map isn't the actual territory.

03:10.560 --> 03:12.720
I mean, there are people who don't care about the edge of the paper.

03:12.720 --> 03:15.440
They just walk off the map and into the unmap silence.

03:15.440 --> 03:16.320
The explorers.

03:16.960 --> 03:21.120
And the author brings up several of these explorers who don't just point at the silence,

03:21.120 --> 03:24.560
but they orient their entire being toward experiencing it.

03:25.360 --> 03:27.440
Like in the West, you have Edmund Husswell,

03:27.440 --> 03:30.320
who developed a contemplative practice called the epicky.

03:30.320 --> 03:30.960
Okay, wait.

03:30.960 --> 03:32.720
How does that actually work in practice?

03:32.720 --> 03:36.480
Because contemplative practice can sound a bit, you know, abstract.

03:36.480 --> 03:40.720
But through it right now, imagine you are holding a standard ceramic coffee cup.

03:40.720 --> 03:41.760
Okay, got it.

03:41.760 --> 03:44.800
Normally, your brain instantly labels it, right?

03:44.800 --> 03:49.120
Coffee cup, use for drinking, holds hot liquid, costs five dollars.

03:49.920 --> 03:55.120
You've applied human utility, history, and economics to it before you even really looked at it.

03:55.120 --> 03:56.320
Right, it's instantaneous.

03:56.320 --> 03:56.880
Exactly.

03:57.600 --> 04:02.000
But Husswell's epique is the rigorous practice of spipping all of that away.

04:02.000 --> 04:05.520
You suspend your judgment about its use, you stop calling it a cup.

04:05.520 --> 04:06.560
So you just look at it.

04:06.560 --> 04:09.120
You just observe the curve of the ceramic,

04:09.120 --> 04:14.480
but the light hits the glaze, the sheer undeniable presence of this object existing in space.

04:15.040 --> 04:17.200
You aren't logically deducing anything.

04:17.200 --> 04:21.520
You are training your attention to experience the raw phenomena

04:21.520 --> 04:23.520
before language gets its hands on it.

04:23.520 --> 04:24.480
Oh, wow.

04:24.480 --> 04:24.960
Yeah.

04:24.960 --> 04:28.240
And Martin Heidegger took a very similar path with his concept of

04:28.240 --> 04:31.840
glass and height or a releasement, which is just letting things be

04:31.840 --> 04:34.720
underneath the human obsession with naming and using them.

04:34.720 --> 04:36.000
That makes a lot of sense.

04:36.000 --> 04:37.920
If they can Stein is drawing the map,

04:37.920 --> 04:41.840
those guys are trying to feel the dirt under their boots in the unmapped woods.

04:41.920 --> 04:45.280
And that clearly echoes Eastern contemplative traditions too,

04:45.280 --> 04:46.480
especially the Buddha.

04:46.480 --> 04:46.960
It does.

04:46.960 --> 04:49.360
And the author highlights a brilliant paradox here.

04:50.240 --> 04:52.480
The Buddha reportedly said on his deathbed,

04:52.480 --> 04:54.000
I have not said a single word.

04:54.000 --> 04:55.040
Which is pretty funny.

04:55.040 --> 04:55.840
Right.

04:55.840 --> 04:59.680
Now, if you look at that through Wittgenstein's purely logical lens,

04:59.680 --> 05:00.640
it's a lie.

05:00.640 --> 05:02.640
I mean, the man taught for 45 years.

05:02.640 --> 05:04.960
There are thousands of pages of discourses.

05:04.960 --> 05:06.560
Yeah, quite a few words, actually.

05:06.560 --> 05:07.120
Exactly.

05:07.680 --> 05:10.080
But from the perspective of pure practice,

05:10.080 --> 05:12.320
he was telling the absolute truth.

05:12.320 --> 05:14.880
The teachings, the words, the concepts,

05:14.880 --> 05:16.960
they were just fingers pointing at the moon.

05:16.960 --> 05:18.320
They are the scaffolding.

05:18.320 --> 05:20.400
You use the metal poles to build the building,

05:20.400 --> 05:22.480
but once the building can stand on its own,

05:22.480 --> 05:23.600
you take the poles away.

05:24.320 --> 05:26.080
The words are just temporary tools.

05:26.080 --> 05:27.120
He's exactly it.

05:27.120 --> 05:28.640
I mean, the Diamond Sutra,

05:28.640 --> 05:30.240
which is a core Buddhist text,

05:30.240 --> 05:31.920
states that all forms are illusion.

05:32.640 --> 05:34.880
The goal isn't to memorize the words.

05:34.880 --> 05:37.440
The goal is what's called direct seeing.

05:37.440 --> 05:37.840
Right.

05:37.840 --> 05:39.520
But since we are using a podcast,

05:40.080 --> 05:42.640
well, an inherently language-based medium

05:42.640 --> 05:44.480
to discuss the limits of language,

05:44.480 --> 05:46.400
we are stuck with the scaffolding for now.

05:46.400 --> 05:46.960
Fair point.

05:46.960 --> 05:48.320
We have to use words.

05:48.320 --> 05:50.800
And because words and inherited cultural concepts

05:50.800 --> 05:52.480
can only take us so far,

05:52.480 --> 05:54.880
we need a method to figure out what is actually real.

05:55.600 --> 05:57.040
If we want to know what to care about,

05:57.040 --> 05:59.760
the author argues we have to bring in a demolition crew.

05:59.760 --> 06:00.880
A demolition crew?

06:00.880 --> 06:02.160
I like the sound of that.

06:02.160 --> 06:03.920
Yes, rationality.

06:04.640 --> 06:08.000
We have to take everything we consider honorable or meaningful

06:08.080 --> 06:10.080
and let the demolition crew a pure reason

06:10.080 --> 06:11.280
take a sledgehammer to it.

06:11.920 --> 06:13.520
We need to clear out the unexamined,

06:13.520 --> 06:16.240
inherited beliefs to see what's left standing.

06:16.240 --> 06:18.560
Okay, let's bring the crew to the job site then.

06:18.560 --> 06:20.080
Because the author targets something

06:20.080 --> 06:22.480
that basically every human society holds a sacred,

06:22.480 --> 06:24.320
which is romantic love.

06:24.320 --> 06:24.960
Oh, yes.

06:24.960 --> 06:26.960
Literature, movies, poetry.

06:27.680 --> 06:30.160
They all tell us that the grand sweeping romance

06:30.160 --> 06:32.560
is the ultimate noble human achievement.

06:32.560 --> 06:34.080
But when you let the demolition crew

06:34.080 --> 06:36.560
of rationality loose on romantic love,

06:36.880 --> 06:38.960
the nobility crumbles pretty fast.

06:38.960 --> 06:40.880
What you are actually looking at is just

06:40.880 --> 06:42.160
evolutionary biology.

06:42.160 --> 06:43.520
Oh, boy, here we go.

06:43.520 --> 06:44.720
Hundreds of millions of years ago,

06:44.720 --> 06:47.040
the specific DNA sequences that disposed

06:47.040 --> 06:49.120
are mammalian ancestors to pair bond

06:49.120 --> 06:51.200
to feel intensely attached to a mate,

06:51.200 --> 06:53.520
where the sequences that successfully procreated

06:53.520 --> 06:56.000
and kept offspring alive long enough to breed.

06:56.000 --> 06:57.520
Okay, logically, I hear you.

06:57.520 --> 07:01.120
The DNA that didn't create that desperate fiery urge

07:01.120 --> 07:02.080
simply died out.

07:02.640 --> 07:06.400
It is a blind, ruthless, mechanical optimization process.

07:06.480 --> 07:07.200
Hold on.

07:07.200 --> 07:08.320
I have to push back on this.

07:08.320 --> 07:08.960
Go for it.

07:08.960 --> 07:11.920
Because when I had a terrible day yesterday,

07:11.920 --> 07:13.760
and my spouse sat with me on the couch,

07:13.760 --> 07:15.600
put an arm around me and just listened,

07:16.560 --> 07:18.960
are you telling me I am supposed to view

07:18.960 --> 07:20.960
that profoundly comforting moment

07:21.600 --> 07:24.000
as just a mammalian survival reflex?

07:24.000 --> 07:24.400
Oh, look.

07:24.400 --> 07:26.400
Because that strips the beauty right out of it.

07:26.400 --> 07:29.600
It makes love sound like a biological algorithm.

07:29.600 --> 07:32.560
And quite frankly, that is incredibly bleak.

07:32.560 --> 07:35.200
It feels bleak because you are looking for the universe

07:35.440 --> 07:37.360
to validate the beauty of that moment.

07:37.360 --> 07:38.000
Dammit.

07:38.000 --> 07:41.600
The author leans on the philosopher David Hune's famous is

07:41.600 --> 07:43.440
Aught, distinction here.

07:43.440 --> 07:47.440
Hume pointed out that you cannot derive an Aught from an is.

07:47.440 --> 07:49.840
Just because love is a blind evolutionary mechanism

07:49.840 --> 07:51.440
doesn't mean it ought to be meaningless to you.

07:51.440 --> 07:52.480
Okay, that's a relief.

07:52.480 --> 07:54.640
But, and this is the heavy swing of the sledgehammer.

07:54.640 --> 07:57.040
The universe doesn't care about your moment on the couch.

07:57.040 --> 07:57.840
Out.

07:57.840 --> 08:01.360
The cosmos is not giving your romance a stamp of approval.

08:01.360 --> 08:04.400
There is no inherent objective cosmic honor to it,

08:04.400 --> 08:06.160
independent of human biology.

08:06.160 --> 08:08.720
So the universe isn't endorsing my marriage.

08:08.720 --> 08:09.360
Fine.

08:09.360 --> 08:11.280
I can mourn the loss of cosmic romance.

08:12.160 --> 08:14.880
But surely we can look at the macro scale like

08:14.880 --> 08:18.400
we honor people who push the entire species forward.

08:18.400 --> 08:22.160
Albert Einstein or Jensen Huang building the architecture for AI.

08:22.160 --> 08:22.640
Right.

08:22.640 --> 08:23.680
Massive figures.

08:23.680 --> 08:26.800
We honor ambition and the flourishing of humanity.

08:26.800 --> 08:28.960
That has to be an objective good.

08:28.960 --> 08:31.520
Let's point the demolition crew at humanism then.

08:31.520 --> 08:34.160
Why is the flourishing of humanity the highest objective

08:34.240 --> 08:35.840
good because we are conscious.

08:35.840 --> 08:36.880
We build art.

08:36.880 --> 08:39.280
We unravel the fizzence of the universe.

08:39.280 --> 08:40.800
We cure diseases.

08:40.800 --> 08:42.880
We also eradicate ecosystems.

08:42.880 --> 08:43.680
Well, true.

08:43.680 --> 08:46.560
The author challenges this inherent bias toward humanism

08:46.560 --> 08:48.880
by asking why not earthism?

08:48.880 --> 08:51.840
Why not prioritize the flourishing of the planet as a whole?

08:51.840 --> 08:53.920
I mean, a lot of people do advocate for that.

08:53.920 --> 08:54.160
Right.

08:54.160 --> 08:56.400
But if you truly adopt earthism,

08:56.400 --> 08:58.880
humanity stops looking like a noble conscious species

08:58.880 --> 09:01.280
and starts looking like a destructive parasite.

09:01.280 --> 09:03.920
The essay specifically references that chilling scene

09:03.920 --> 09:05.680
with agent Smith and the matrix

09:05.680 --> 09:08.640
where he looks at Morpheus and says human beings are a virus.

09:08.640 --> 09:10.000
Oh, I remember that.

09:10.000 --> 09:11.440
We move to an area.

09:11.440 --> 09:14.880
Multiply until every natural resource is consumed.

09:14.880 --> 09:18.320
And the only way we survive is to spread to another area.

09:19.280 --> 09:21.120
From an earthism perspective,

09:21.120 --> 09:23.920
wiping out humanity would be the most honorable thing you could do.

09:23.920 --> 09:25.520
Okay, that escalated quickly.

09:25.520 --> 09:27.280
Or consider alienism.

09:27.280 --> 09:30.720
If consciousness and problem solving are the metrics for nobility,

09:31.280 --> 09:34.880
shouldn't we prioritize the flourishing of much older,

09:34.880 --> 09:36.800
vastly more intelligent life forms

09:36.800 --> 09:38.800
that might exist elsewhere in the galaxy?

09:38.800 --> 09:40.480
Right, because our human effubence

09:40.480 --> 09:42.160
would just look like an ant farm to them.

09:42.160 --> 09:43.200
Exactly.

09:43.200 --> 09:46.000
What's fascinating here is that you can't use rationality

09:46.000 --> 09:49.520
to definitively prove that humanism is objectively better

09:49.520 --> 09:51.280
than earthism or alienism.

09:51.280 --> 09:54.240
Because every time you try to plant a flag and say,

09:54.240 --> 09:55.440
this is the ultimate good.

09:55.440 --> 09:57.600
The demolition crew just knocks it down.

09:57.600 --> 09:59.360
Every single standpoint dissolves

09:59.360 --> 10:01.040
under the pressure of pure rationality.

10:01.760 --> 10:04.960
Rational logic requires foundational premises to function,

10:05.680 --> 10:07.520
but it cannot prove its own premises.

10:08.160 --> 10:10.720
Eventually, you run out of floorboards, you hit the void.

10:10.720 --> 10:11.520
The void.

10:11.520 --> 10:14.480
Yeah, there is absolutely no cosmic foundation that says

10:14.480 --> 10:16.240
anything is inherently honorable.

10:16.240 --> 10:18.400
Which brings up a massive problem.

10:18.400 --> 10:21.840
If the demolition crew of rationality is this destructive,

10:22.480 --> 10:23.840
why hire them at all?

10:23.840 --> 10:27.120
Why use a tool that destroys everything we hold dear

10:27.120 --> 10:31.520
and leaves us standing in a terrifying, meaningless cosmic void?

10:31.520 --> 10:35.840
The author values rationality for purely pragmatic reasons.

10:35.840 --> 10:37.680
I mean, you don't hire a demolition crew

10:37.680 --> 10:39.200
because you love the sight of rubble.

10:39.200 --> 10:39.680
Right.

10:39.680 --> 10:41.680
You hire them because the building currently on the lot

10:41.680 --> 10:44.080
is full of toxic mold and rotting wood.

10:44.080 --> 10:44.560
Yeah.

10:44.560 --> 10:46.640
Are inherited cultural programming.

10:46.640 --> 10:49.280
The arbitrary rules about what success looks like,

10:49.280 --> 10:51.760
who we are supposed to love, how we are supposed to work it,

10:51.760 --> 10:53.840
can trap us in lives we don't actually want.

10:53.840 --> 10:55.360
That is painfully true.

10:55.440 --> 10:57.520
Rationality clears the lot.

10:57.520 --> 11:00.720
It clears away the illusions so that we can clearly see reality.

11:00.720 --> 11:03.200
If you rely on unexamined emotional reasoning,

11:03.200 --> 11:07.600
you just blindly repeat whatever your surrounding culture told you was honorable.

11:07.600 --> 11:11.680
I see the utility, but here's the existential dread I'm feeling right now.

11:11.680 --> 11:12.160
Oh.

11:12.160 --> 11:14.400
The demolition crew did a fantastic job.

11:14.400 --> 11:15.760
They leveled the toxic building.

11:15.760 --> 11:18.080
The cultural illusions are gone,

11:18.080 --> 11:21.360
but now I am standing alone in a perfectly clean,

11:21.360 --> 11:23.040
totally empty dirt lot.

11:23.040 --> 11:24.160
And it's cold.

11:24.240 --> 11:25.360
It's freezing out here.

11:25.360 --> 11:26.880
The universe doesn't care about me.

11:26.880 --> 11:28.560
Nihilism is setting in.

11:28.560 --> 11:30.880
And I can't live in an empty dirt lot.

11:30.880 --> 11:32.240
And you don't have to.

11:32.240 --> 11:33.840
The demolition crew's job is done.

11:34.560 --> 11:36.160
Now you bring in the architect.

11:36.160 --> 11:36.960
Wisdom.

11:36.960 --> 11:37.760
Wisdom.

11:37.760 --> 11:40.320
This is the most crucial pivot in the essay, the return.

11:41.280 --> 11:44.320
The author visits the void, sees the empty dirt

11:44.320 --> 11:46.960
lawn, realizes there was no cosmic meaning,

11:46.960 --> 11:48.400
and then comes back to the human.

11:48.400 --> 11:49.120
Comes back.

11:49.120 --> 11:51.920
Yes, you go back to emotions, back to love,

11:51.920 --> 11:54.000
back to your family, your ambitions,

11:54.000 --> 11:55.440
the messy reality of the world.

11:55.440 --> 11:57.840
But I don't understand the logic of the return.

11:57.840 --> 12:00.240
If the demolition crew just definitively prove

12:00.240 --> 12:01.840
that love is a biological glitch

12:01.840 --> 12:03.760
and ambition is a cultural illusion,

12:03.760 --> 12:05.360
why on earth would I go back to them?

12:05.360 --> 12:06.160
Because you were human.

12:06.880 --> 12:08.720
That is the bare, inescapable,

12:08.720 --> 12:10.880
physical reality of your existence.

12:10.880 --> 12:12.960
You aren't some abstract floating consciousness.

12:12.960 --> 12:16.160
You are a biological creature thrown into a human body.

12:16.160 --> 12:17.520
Right, I can just float away.

12:17.520 --> 12:18.640
Exactly.

12:18.640 --> 12:21.200
These emotions, these messy attachments,

12:21.760 --> 12:23.440
they are the only building materials

12:23.440 --> 12:24.960
you actually have in that dirt lot.

12:25.600 --> 12:28.640
You don't value them because the universe demands it.

12:29.280 --> 12:32.240
You value them because it is the only ground you can stand on.

12:32.240 --> 12:33.520
So it isn't a retreat.

12:33.520 --> 12:35.920
I'm not closing my eyes, pretending I never saw the empty void

12:35.920 --> 12:38.640
and trying to rebuild the exact same toxic house.

12:38.640 --> 12:40.880
No, it is eyes wide open choice.

12:41.360 --> 12:43.680
The essay connects this architect mindset

12:43.680 --> 12:47.520
to Aristotelian Fernesis, which is practical wisdom.

12:47.520 --> 12:49.040
Ah, right.

12:49.040 --> 12:52.480
It's also Sartre's concept of the existential choice.

12:52.480 --> 12:54.400
You are condemned to be free,

12:54.400 --> 12:56.400
so you must choose to construct meaning.

12:56.400 --> 12:57.520
Because no one else will.

12:57.520 --> 12:58.720
Exactly.

12:58.720 --> 13:00.000
In Mahayana Buddhism,

13:00.000 --> 13:03.040
profound compassion arises spontaneously.

13:03.040 --> 13:05.600
But only after the practitioner fully recognizes

13:05.600 --> 13:07.360
the ultimate emptiness of all things.

13:07.920 --> 13:11.040
Wisdom is knowing exactly when to send the demolition crew home

13:11.040 --> 13:12.080
and start laying bricks.

13:12.640 --> 13:15.280
The author brings in Sir and Kierkegaard's concept

13:15.280 --> 13:16.480
of the night of faith here.

13:16.480 --> 13:17.680
And I want to spend a second on this

13:17.680 --> 13:20.240
because it explains the mechanics of how this actually feels.

13:20.240 --> 13:21.440
Yeah, it's a great concept.

13:21.440 --> 13:23.520
Kierkegaard describes a double movement.

13:24.160 --> 13:26.160
First, there is infinite resignation.

13:26.720 --> 13:28.640
You stand in the dirt lot.

13:28.640 --> 13:32.160
You fully, painfully surrender every single attachment

13:32.160 --> 13:33.520
you have to the world,

13:33.520 --> 13:36.960
deeply internalizing that none of it has ultimate cosmic backing.

13:36.960 --> 13:38.480
You let the ego shatter.

13:38.480 --> 13:38.720
Yes.

13:39.600 --> 13:42.080
But the second movement is the total return.

13:42.080 --> 13:43.920
You walk back into your normal life

13:43.920 --> 13:47.200
and you love it fully as if it had cosmic meaning

13:47.200 --> 13:50.080
while simultaneously holding the knowledge that it doesn't.

13:50.080 --> 13:50.480
Yeah.

13:50.480 --> 13:53.360
Like, think about watching a toddler hand you a drawing they made.

13:53.360 --> 13:55.200
It's just scribbles on cheap paper.

13:55.200 --> 13:56.160
Barely legible.

13:56.160 --> 13:57.120
Exactly.

13:57.120 --> 14:00.080
The cosmos does not care about this piece of paper.

14:00.080 --> 14:03.600
A rational demolition crew would tell you it's worthless pulp.

14:03.600 --> 14:04.880
But you choose to frame it.

14:04.880 --> 14:07.040
You choose to assign it infinite value.

14:07.040 --> 14:09.920
Total release, followed by total return.

14:09.920 --> 14:12.960
Without the release, your return is just naive attachment.

14:12.960 --> 14:16.080
You are just an organism following its biological programming,

14:16.080 --> 14:18.640
getting stressed about things that don't ultimately matter.

14:18.640 --> 14:22.240
But without the return, the release is entirely sterile.

14:22.240 --> 14:25.040
You're just a cynic, sitting alone in the dirt lot,

14:25.760 --> 14:27.520
refusing to play the game of life.

14:27.520 --> 14:29.840
But who is playing the game?

14:29.840 --> 14:32.640
Because this raises a huge mechanical question for me.

14:32.640 --> 14:33.280
Okay, what is it?

14:33.840 --> 14:37.040
If the ego shattered during the first movement,

14:37.040 --> 14:40.960
if we've completely surrendered our unexamined attachments

14:40.960 --> 14:45.760
and recognized that our individual identity is just a biological story,

14:45.760 --> 14:48.560
who exactly is making the choice to return?

14:49.280 --> 14:51.760
Who is the architect designing the new house?

14:52.400 --> 14:56.880
That leads us directly into the Buddha's concept of anata, or no self.

14:57.440 --> 15:00.400
And we really need to clear up a massive misconception about this.

15:00.400 --> 15:01.440
Yes, please do.

15:01.440 --> 15:06.640
People hear no self, and they assume it means becoming a passive apathetic zombie.

15:06.640 --> 15:10.720
They think, if there is no me, then nobody is home to make choices.

15:10.720 --> 15:13.200
You just drift through life, letting things happen to you.

15:13.200 --> 15:15.360
Yeah, it sounds like giving up your agency entirely.

15:15.440 --> 15:17.760
The reality of the practice is the exact opposite.

15:17.760 --> 15:20.320
When you lose the rigid, bounded self,

15:20.320 --> 15:22.080
your agency doesn't disappear.

15:22.080 --> 15:23.120
It becomes unbounded.

15:23.120 --> 15:23.840
Unbounded.

15:23.840 --> 15:26.320
Think about how much of your daily cognitive energy is spent

15:26.320 --> 15:28.800
defending your tiny fragile ego.

15:28.800 --> 15:30.720
We are constantly anxious about how we look,

15:30.720 --> 15:32.160
protective of our status,

15:32.160 --> 15:34.080
furious when someone cuts us off in traffic.

15:34.080 --> 15:35.280
Exhausting, honestly.

15:35.280 --> 15:36.080
It is.

15:36.080 --> 15:38.480
Our wanting is incredibly small and defensive.

15:39.760 --> 15:41.520
If we connect this to the bigger picture,

15:41.520 --> 15:44.080
when the demolition crew destroys that small self,

15:44.800 --> 15:46.640
the energy of wanting doesn't vanish.

15:47.360 --> 15:50.960
The wanting of the small ego transforms into the wanting of the whole.

15:50.960 --> 15:52.320
Unbounded agency.

15:52.320 --> 15:52.640
Okay.

15:52.640 --> 15:55.280
Let's translate this to normal every day life.

15:55.280 --> 15:56.800
The essay talks about the body stuff,

15:56.800 --> 15:58.160
they're returning to the marketplace.

15:58.160 --> 15:58.720
Right.

15:58.720 --> 16:02.080
But the marketplace isn't some dusty, ancient bizarre.

16:02.080 --> 16:04.400
The marketplace is a 45-minute commute

16:04.400 --> 16:06.160
in bumper to bumper traffic.

16:06.160 --> 16:08.960
It's dealing with a passive-aggressive co-worker.

16:08.960 --> 16:12.800
It's waking up at 3 a.m. to change a diaper when you are exhausted.

16:12.880 --> 16:14.640
That is the modern marketplace.

16:14.640 --> 16:15.840
And the unpounded agent,

16:15.840 --> 16:18.720
the bodhisattva steps right into the middle of that mess,

16:18.720 --> 16:20.880
not out of a grudging sense of biological duty,

16:20.880 --> 16:22.400
but out of total free choice.

16:22.400 --> 16:23.040
Wow.

16:23.040 --> 16:25.520
The bodhisattva acts for the benefit of all sentient beings,

16:25.520 --> 16:28.000
because having laws the boundary of the ego,

16:28.000 --> 16:31.200
they recognize there is no actual separation between themselves

16:31.200 --> 16:32.880
and the annoyed driver next to them,

16:32.880 --> 16:34.240
or the crying baby.

16:34.240 --> 16:35.840
Here's where it gets really interesting.

16:35.840 --> 16:38.640
It fundamentally changes the texture of the action.

16:39.440 --> 16:44.000
When you step back into the messy, chaotic human world,

16:44.000 --> 16:45.680
you aren't doing it out of cowardice

16:45.680 --> 16:47.760
because the cosmic void was too scary.

16:47.760 --> 16:49.040
Oh, not at all.

16:49.040 --> 16:51.600
You are stepping forward to care for the world

16:51.600 --> 16:53.360
because you are free.

16:53.360 --> 16:55.440
You are building meaning from scratch.

16:55.440 --> 16:57.600
You are extending your hands into the marketplace

16:57.600 --> 17:01.520
because that is simply what an unbounded consciousness does

17:01.520 --> 17:04.000
when it finds itself housed in a human body.

17:04.000 --> 17:04.880
It connects.

17:04.880 --> 17:05.680
It builds.

17:05.680 --> 17:06.480
It helps.

17:06.480 --> 17:08.640
In this journey, we've just taken completely

17:08.640 --> 17:10.800
reframes the core problem we started with.

17:10.800 --> 17:11.520
It really does.

17:11.520 --> 17:14.160
We began this deep dive looking for the right question

17:14.160 --> 17:16.160
to ask about our life's purpose.

17:16.160 --> 17:19.120
We went from Vitgenstein, drawing the limits of logic,

17:19.120 --> 17:21.760
to bringing in the demolition crew of rationality,

17:21.760 --> 17:24.320
to tear down romantic love and humanism.

17:24.320 --> 17:25.920
All the way to the dirt lot of the void.

17:25.920 --> 17:26.720
Exactly.

17:26.720 --> 17:29.280
And finally, we met the architect of wisdom,

17:29.280 --> 17:32.160
making the double movement back into the modern marketplace.

17:32.800 --> 17:35.600
The author proves with rigorous clarity

17:35.680 --> 17:38.160
that looking up at the sky and asking the universe

17:38.160 --> 17:42.480
is anything honorable, is fundamentally a broken question.

17:42.480 --> 17:45.520
Because the cosmos is utterly silent,

17:45.520 --> 17:47.440
demanding that the universe provide you

17:47.440 --> 17:50.320
with a hidden list of honorable things to care about

17:50.320 --> 17:53.360
is essentially dodging your own responsibility.

17:53.360 --> 17:56.320
It's asking someone else to build your house for you.

17:56.320 --> 17:58.080
The only question that holds any water is,

17:58.960 --> 18:00.720
do I choose to honor anything?

18:01.440 --> 18:03.600
The agency is entirely yours.

18:04.240 --> 18:06.000
You are the engine of meaning.

18:06.000 --> 18:07.920
You are the one doing the honoring

18:07.920 --> 18:10.240
without any safety net or cosmic guarantee.

18:10.800 --> 18:13.760
The essay ends with a final nod to the diamond sutra,

18:13.760 --> 18:16.080
summarizing this exact state of mind.

18:16.080 --> 18:19.120
It says, let the mind arise without dwelling anywhere.

18:19.120 --> 18:20.240
You definitely said.

18:20.240 --> 18:24.080
You don't rigidly cling to the poxic house of inherited beliefs,

18:24.080 --> 18:27.440
but you also refuse to dwell in the empty cynical dirt

18:27.440 --> 18:28.640
lot of nihilism.

18:28.640 --> 18:31.040
Your mind arises freely, engaged deeply

18:31.040 --> 18:33.440
with whatever is right in front of you fully present.

18:33.440 --> 18:34.960
Is the ultimate creative act?

18:34.960 --> 18:36.640
So to you, listening to this right now,

18:36.640 --> 18:38.160
if you have been carrying around that heavy,

18:38.160 --> 18:41.040
paralyzing anxiety, trying to discover your objective,

18:41.040 --> 18:42.800
pre-run purpose, put it down.

18:42.800 --> 18:43.600
So drop it.

18:43.600 --> 18:45.520
The universe didn't give you a purpose,

18:45.520 --> 18:47.520
but it gave you something infinitely better.

18:47.520 --> 18:49.920
It gave you the terrifying, beautiful,

18:49.920 --> 18:51.920
freedom to build one yourself,

18:51.920 --> 18:53.600
the capacity to honor things,

18:53.600 --> 18:54.800
to find things meaningful,

18:54.800 --> 18:57.360
is a muscle you flex entirely by choice.

18:57.360 --> 18:58.080
Absolutely.

18:58.080 --> 19:00.160
What is one mundane,

19:00.160 --> 19:02.400
everyday thing in your life right now

19:02.400 --> 19:04.720
that you have been treating as just background noise,

19:04.720 --> 19:08.240
that you could actively, deliberately choose to honor today?

19:08.240 --> 19:10.160
Because remember, the lot is cleared,

19:10.160 --> 19:11.920
the materials are in your hands,

19:11.920 --> 19:13.600
and if you don't build the meaning,

19:13.600 --> 19:15.600
no one else is going to do it for you.

